{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"C\n    \n      Hybrid — Shared UI + Platform Modules (SPM)\n      Each platform is a Swift Package. A thin orchestrator picks the right package at runtime. UI is shared but can be extended per platform.\n      \n        Pros\n          Best modularity — platforms can be developed/tested in isolation\n          SPM packages can be reused in other apps\n          Build times improve — only compile what you need\n        \n        Cons\n          Most complex setup\n          Package dependency management overhead\n          Overkill if you're a solo developer","choice":"c","id":null,"timestamp":1775580823002}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"A\n    \n      Protocol Abstraction Layer (Recommended)\n      A unified TVProtocol interface with platform-specific drivers behind it. The UI talks to one API — the driver handles platform differences.\n      \n        Pros\n          Clean separation — UI never knows which platform it's talking to\n          Adding a new platform = adding a new driver, no UI changes\n          Easy to test each driver independently\n          Context-adaptive UI works naturally — drivers report TV state uniformly\n        \n        Cons\n          Upfront abstraction design takes thought\n          Some platform-specific features may not fit the common interface","choice":"a","id":null,"timestamp":1775580830045}
